a thirst to study amidst the lockdown  and under the shadow of the coronavirus  these children from the poorest of the  poor gather every day  as if it was regular classes  a makeshift school run by satinderpal  shakya  a university student himself has come  most  handy during the pandemic sitting on  rugs these children are unperturbed by  the banging of heavy equipment  around them the under construction  flyover also protects them from the  elements  schools have been shut since march and  all learning is to be done by online  classes  but for the millions of poor children  it's a privilege  they cannot afford  [Music]  [Music]  the digital divide is stark even in the  nation's capital  most of india lives in its villages  with such facilities are scarce and  lacking  a fear that many may be left out of  education  14 year old rahul is in grade 10 a  crucial year  he's seen his grades in maths and  sciences improve  by these offline classes  so  the only covered classroom is full  some semblance of school the months of  lockdown of educational institutions and  schools  across this country has come out with  its own complexities  a digital divide where millions of  children  of marginalized communities have borne  the brunt on their education  after class rahul is back home to help  his father in the fields  a sheikh cropper dayaram just about  manages to eke out a living  from his patch the contagion has drained  him of all his savings  unable to provide for his children's  digital classes  for the moment the free school is the  only saving grace  neville lazarus sky news delhi
 

